  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes major engineering and safety issues for electrochemical systems that use gases and liquid electrolytes, comparing non‑aqueous and aqueous designs and linking them to key hazards. At the top, gas management is shown as a central challenge. In non‑aqueous cells, a sealed cell with a valve handles CO2 and other generated gases. In aqueous systems, a more complex flow cell is illustrated with catholyte and anolyte circuits, pumps, a membrane, gas outlets, and a CO2 inlet, emphasizing how gaseous reactants and products such as H2, CO, CH4, and CO2 must be supplied, separated, and collected alongside liquid products.\n\nThe lower panels highlight three main risks. Gas safety focuses on toxic or flammable gases, showing that gas evolution can lead to explosion or inhalation hazards. Thermal runaway is linked to mechanical abuse, electrical abuse, and exothermic reactions, with a schematic of one hot cell triggering neighboring cells, stressing propagation through a battery pack. Corrosive electrolytes are presented as another concern, especially HF formation and concentrated alkaline solutions, which can cause electrolyte leakage and material damage.\n\nOverall, the graphic conveys that practical deployment depends not only on electrochemical performance but also on managing gases, preventing runaway, and controlling corrosion and leakage.",
